cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or cite these results, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "market-regime-detection: does a regime model forecast volatility better than the simple measures it competes with?"
authors:
  - family-names: Quinn
    given-names: Thomas
    affiliation: "University College Dublin"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-10-17
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Thomas-quinn7/market-regime-detection"
abstract: >-
  A two-state Markov-switching model of equity-index volatility, estimated walk-forward with no
  look-ahead, tested against a HAR regression plus the asset's implied volatility index on four
  holdouts whose protocols were committed and pushed before the holdout data was opened, each run
  once. Adding the regime probability to HAR plus implied volatility never changed the forecast
  error measurably; the regime models' own forecasts lost to HAR plus implied volatility on every
  holdout but one, where they were level. Richer regime models (Markov-switching multifractal,
  Markov-switching GARCH) beat the two-state model in places and still lost to the same baseline.
  These verdicts are per comparison; most individual wins and losses do not survive a Holm correction
  across the 81 registered comparisons, which cannot change the main null result.
keywords:
  - volatility forecasting
  - Markov-switching
  - regime detection
  - HAR
  - implied volatility
  - pre-registered holdout
